<article class="env-lemma" id="01G4">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/01G4">Lemma <span data-tag="01G4">25.3.2</span>.</a> Let $\mathcal{C}$ be a site. </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
A composition of coverings in $\text{SR}(\mathcal{C})$ is a covering. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
If $K \to L$ is a covering in $\text{SR}(\mathcal{C})$ and $L' \to L$ is a morphism, then $L' \times _ L K$ exists and $L' \times _ L K \to L'$ is a covering. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
If $\mathcal{C}$ has products of pairs, and $A \to B$ and $K \to L$ are coverings in $\text{SR}(\mathcal{C})$, then $A \times K \to B \times L$ is a covering. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
  <p> Let $X \in \mathop{\mathrm{Ob}}\nolimits (\mathcal{C})$. Then (1) and (2) holds for $\text{SR}(\mathcal{C}, X)$ and (3) holds if $\mathcal{C}$ has fibre products. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Part (1) is immediate from the axioms of a site. Part (2) follows by the construction of fibre products in $\text{SR}(\mathcal{C})$ in the proof of Lemma <a href="/tag/01G2" data-tag="01G2">25.2.3</a> and the requirement that the morphisms in a covering of $\mathcal{C}$ are representable. Part (3) follows by thinking of $A \times K \to B \times L$ as the composition $A \times K \to B \times K \to B \times L$ and hence a composition of basechanges of coverings. The final statement follows because $\text{SR}(\mathcal{C}, X) = \text{SR}(\mathcal{C}/X)$. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>